            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The 19th-Century Social Crisis &amp; Intellectual Awakening</h3>\n<p>At the dawn of the 19th century, Indian society was gripped by deep socio-religious decay. Orthodoxy, rigid caste discrimination, untouchability, child marriage, female infanticide, and the barbaric burning of living widows (<em>Sati Daha</em>) had paralyzed intellectual dynamism. The impact of Western science, humanism, and British colonial rule stimulated a soul-searching introspection among enlightened Indians, giving birth to the <strong>Indian Renaissance</strong>.</p>\n\n<h3>2. Raja Ram Mohan Roy: The Morning Star of Modern India</h3>\n<p><strong>Raja Ram Mohan Roy</strong> (1772–1833), born into an orthodox Brahmin family at Radhanagar in Bengal, is celebrated as the <em>'Father of Indian Renaissance'</em> and <em>'First Modern Man of India'</em>:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Rational Monotheism:</strong> Roy mastered Sanskrit, Arabic, Persian, English, Latin, Greek, and Hebrew. In 1803, he published his philosophical treatise in Persian, <strong>Tuhfat-ul-Muwahhidin</strong> (<em>A Gift to Deists</em>), with an Arabic introduction, arguing that belief in a single Supreme Being is the universal natural religion of humanity, while miracles, priesthood, and idolatry were human corruptions.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Atmiya Sabha (1814):</strong> After retiring from the East India Company's service in 1814, Roy settled in Calcutta and formed the <strong>Atmiya Sabha</strong> (Society of Friends). It served as an intellectual salon to debate the abolition of idol worship, polytheism, and caste bigotry through readings from the Upanishads.</li>\n  <li><strong>Precepts of Jesus (1820):</strong> In 1820, he published <em>The Precepts of Jesus: The Guide to Peace and Happiness</em>, extracting the moral and ethical teachings of Christ while rejecting Christian dogmas of miracles and the Trinity, earning hostility from orthodox Trinitarian missionaries at Serampore.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Crusades for Social Reform &amp; Abolition of Sati</h3>\n<p>In 1811, Roy witnessed his own sister-in-law forcibly burned alive on the funeral pyre of his brother Jagamohan. Horrified, he resolved to eradicate this barbarity:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Scriptural Counter-Attack:</strong> Roy published pamphlets proving that Sati was never mandated by sacred Hindu scriptures (Manu or the Vedas). He organized volunteer vigilante groups to visit cremation grounds to dissuade widows and relatives.</li>\n  <li><strong>Regulation XVII of 1829:</strong> His courageous advocacy persuaded Governor-General <strong>Lord William Bentinck</strong> to enact <strong>Regulation XVII on 4 December 1829</strong>, declaring Sati illegal and punishable as culpable homicide in Bengal (extended to Madras and Bombay in 1830). When orthodox Hindus petitioned the Privy Council in London to overturn the ban, Roy traveled to England to successfully defend the regulation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Pioneer of Free Press &amp; Education:</strong> Roy founded the Bengali weekly <strong>Sambad Kaumudi</strong> (1821) and the Persian weekly <strong>Mirat-ul-Akhbar</strong> (1822). In 1817, he collaborated with clockmaker <strong>David Hare</strong> to establish the historic <strong>Hindu College</strong> in Calcutta to disseminate secular Western sciences. In 1825, he established the <strong>Vedanta College</strong> to synthesize Indian spiritual monotheism with modern physical sciences.</li>\n  <li><strong>Final Years:</strong> Mughal Emperor Akbar II conferred upon him the royal title of <strong>Raja</strong> in 1830 and dispatched him to London as imperial envoy to represent his pension claims. Roy passed away of meningitis at <strong>Bristol (England)</strong> on 27 September 1833, where his mausoleum stands today at Arnos Vale Cemetery.</li>\n</ol>\n",
