            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Foundation of the Brahmo Samaj (1828)</h3>\n<p>On <strong>20 August 1828</strong>, Raja Ram Mohan Roy, together with his close friend, the merchant prince <strong>Dwarkanath Tagore</strong>, established the <strong>Brahmo Sabha</strong> in a rented house on Chitpur Road in Calcutta. It was soon renamed the <strong>Brahmo Samaj</strong> (The Society of the Adorers of the One Supreme Brahman):</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The Trust Deed of 1830:</strong> In January 1830, the first dedicated Brahmo building was consecrated. The monumental Trust Deed drafted by Roy declared that the building was open to all human beings without distinction of caste, color, or creed, for the worship of the <em>'Eternal, Unsearchable, and Immutable Being, who is the Author and Preserver of the Universe'</em>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Doctrinal Strictures:</strong> The Trust Deed explicitly prohibited any statue, sculpted image, picture, portrait, animal sacrifice, or consumption of meat inside the hall. No religion or sect could be vilified or spoken of contemptuously.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. Debendranath Tagore &amp; The Tattvabodhini Sabha (1839)</h3>\n<p>Following Ram Mohan Roy's death in Bristol in 1833, the Brahmo Samaj languished for almost a decade. It was revived and given permanent institutional vitality by <strong>Debendranath Tagore</strong> (1817–1905), reverently remembered as <strong>Maharshi</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>The Tattvabodhini Sabha:</strong> On 6 October 1839, Debendranath founded the <em>Tattvabodhini Sabha</em> (Truth-Propagating Society) with ten friends at Jorasanko. Its objective was to counter Christian missionary propaganda by investigating and disseminating the sublime monotheistic truths of ancient Indian scriptures and promoting Bengali literature.</li>\n  <li><strong>Tattvabodhini Patrika (1843):</strong> In 1843, the Sabha launched its celebrated Bengali monthly journal, the <strong>Tattvabodhini Patrika</strong>. Edited by the brilliant rationalist <strong>Akshay Kumar Dutt</strong>, the journal became the premier organ of progressive Bengali intellectual thought, promoting scientific inquiry, historical research, and peasant rights.</li>\n  <li><strong>Merger with Brahmo Samaj (1842–1843):</strong> In 1842, Debendranath formally joined the Brahmo Samaj, bringing the vibrant membership of the Tattvabodhini Sabha into its fold. On 21 December 1843, Debendranath and twenty companions took the solemn <em>Brahmo Covenant</em> (a formal pledge of monotheistic faith), transforming the Brahmo Samaj into a structured church with regular congregational worship, ordained ministers, and prayer services.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>3. Akshay Kumar Dutt: Rationalism Over Infallibility</h3>\n<p>Under the intellectual leadership of <strong>Akshay Kumar Dutt</strong>, the Brahmo Samaj took a decisive leap toward scientific rationalism. In 1850, after rigorous scriptural scrutiny, Debendranath and the Samaj abandoned the doctrine of <strong>Vedic Infallibility</strong>. They declared that no scripture—not even the sacred Vedas or Upanishads—was infallible; rather, human reason, conscience, and observed nature were the ultimate arbiters of spiritual truth.</p>\n",
