            "title": "Keshub Chandra Sen, Brahmo Splits & Prarthana Samaj (Atmaram Pandurang, Ranade)",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The Meteoric Rise of Keshub Chandra Sen</h3>\n<p>In 1857, a dynamic, charismatic twenty-year-old youth named <strong>Keshub Chandra Sen</strong> joined the Brahmo Samaj. Recognizing his extraordinary oratorical gifts, Debendranath Tagore appointed him <strong>Acharya</strong> (Minister) of the Samaj in 1862. Keshub rapidly transformed the Samaj from a localized Calcutta gathering into an all-India spiritual phenomenon:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>All-India Expansion:</strong> Keshub toured Bombay, Madras, and Northern India, delivering electrifying English speeches. His tours led directly to the establishment of the <em>Veda Samaj</em> in Madras (1864, later Brahmo Samaj of South India under Sridharalu Naidu) and the <em>Prarthana Samaj</em> in Bombay (1867).</li>\n  <li><strong>Radical Social Program:</strong> Keshub took the movement far beyond Debendranath's cautious boundaries: he threw open the pulpit to non-Brahmins, discarded the Brahminical sacred thread (<em>paita</em>), conducted inter-caste and widow marriages, and introduced readings from the Bible, the Quran, and the Zend Avesta into weekly services.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. The Two Great Schisms of the Brahmo Samaj</h3>\n<p>Keshub's radical cosmopolitanism precipitated two successive structural splits in the Brahmo movement:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>The First Schism (1866):</strong> Debendranath Tagore, wishing to keep the movement rooted in the Hindu Upanishadic heritage, dismissed Keshub from the trusteeship. In November 1866, Keshub broke away to establish the <strong>Brahmo Samaj of India</strong> (incorporating Vaishnavite kirtan and congregational singing). Debendranath's original conservative branch came to be known as the <strong>Adi Brahmo Samaj</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Native Marriage Act of 1872 (Act III of 1872):</strong> Pushed vigorously by Keshub, the colonial government passed this landmark civil marriage legislation. It legalized inter-caste and inter-religious marriages for non-conformists, banned polygamy, and established the legal minimum age of marriage at <strong>14 for females and 18 for males</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Second Schism (May 1878 - The Cooch Behar Marriage):</strong> In 1878, Keshub shockingly agreed to marry his 13-year-old eldest daughter, Suniti Devi, to the minor Maharaja of Cooch Behar (aged 15) with orthodox Brahminical idolatrous rituals. Disgusted by this blatant betrayal of his own 1872 Marriage Act, disgusted young radicals—including <strong>Anandamohan Bose</strong>, <strong>Pandit Sivanath Sastri</strong>, and <strong>Dwarkanath Ganguly</strong>—seceded and formed the democratic <strong>Sadharan Brahmo Samaj</strong> on 15 May 1878. Keshub was left with a mystical, personalized sect termed the <em>Naba Bidhan</em> (New Dispensation).</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>3. The Prarthana Samaj of Maharashtra (1867)</h3>\n<p>Inspired by Keshub's visit to Bombay in 1864 and 1867, <strong>Dr. Atmaram Pandurang</strong> founded the <strong>Prarthana Samaj</strong> (Prayer Society) on 31 March 1867:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The Ranade Era:</strong> In 1870, <strong>Justice Mahadev Govind Ranade</strong> and distinguished Sanskrit scholar <strong>Dr. R.G. Bhandarkar</strong> joined the Samaj, elevating it to great intellectual and social stature.</li>\n  <li><strong>Core Philosophy:</strong> Unlike the Brahmo Samaj, the Prarthana Samaj did not establish a separate religious community outside orthodox Hinduism. It anchored its monotheism in the beloved domestic Marathi <strong>Bhakti tradition</strong> of Saint Tukaram, Namdev, and Eknath.</li>\n  <li><strong>Fourfold Practical Program:</strong> Rather than attacking theological dogmas, Ranade directed energy toward four constructive social tasks:\n    <ol>\n      <li>Disapproval of caste exclusiveness and untouchability.</li>\n      <li>Promotion of female education.</li>\n      <li>Support for widow remarriage (Ranade founded the <em>Widow Remarriage Association</em> in 1861).</li>\n      <li>Raising the age of marriage for both boys and girls.</li>\n    </ol>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Depressed Classes Mission:</strong> Under Samaj stalwart <strong>Vitthal Ramji Shinde (V.R. Shinde)</strong>, the <em>Depressed Classes Mission Society of India</em> was established in 1906 to provide free schools, technical institutes, and hostels for the Dalits.</li>\n</ul>\n",
