            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Swami Dayananda Saraswati: The Vedic Iconoclast</h3>\n<p>Born into an orthodox Brahmin family in Gujarat as <strong>Moolshankar Tiwari</strong>, young Dayananda fled home to avoid an arranged marriage. Wandering as an ascetic across India for fifteen years, he found his spiritual preceptor in the blind Vedic master, <strong>Swami Virjananda</strong> of Mathura. At the end of his training, Virjananda asked for one fee (<em>guru-dakshina</em>): <em>'Vow to dedicate your life to dispelling Vedic ignorance and eradicating false dogmas.'</em></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>'Go Back to the Vedas!'</strong> Dayananda declared that the four original Samhitas of the <strong>Vedas</strong> were infallible divine revelations of cosmic truth, pure monotheism, and natural sciences. He rejected all later Puranas, Tantras, and Smritis as corrupted human fabrications designed by self-serving priests to deceive the masses.</li>\n  <li><strong>Satyarth Prakash (1875):</strong> His magnum opus, written in clear, accessible Hindi, systematically dismantled idol worship, ritual pilgrimages (<em>tirthas</em>), caste by birth (insisting caste was determined purely by merit, virtue, and character—<em>Guna, Karma, Swabhava</em>), and animal sacrifices.</li>\n  <li><strong>Pioneer of Swaraj &amp; Swadeshi:</strong> Long before the Congress adopted the goal, Dayananda declared that <em>'Foreign rule, however benevolent or paternal, can never equal indigenous self-government (Swaraj).'</em> He championed Swadeshi products and advocated Hindi as the national link language of India.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. The Arya Samaj (1875) &amp; Shuddhi Movement</h3>\n<p>On <strong>10 April 1875</strong>, Dayananda established the <strong>Arya Samaj</strong> at Bombay, shifting its dynamic headquarters to <strong>Lahore</strong> in 1877, where it became an unstoppable socio-religious force in the Punjab and UP:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>The Ten Principles:</strong> Inculcated devotion to the One Formless God, daily performance of the simple Vedic <em>Havan/Yajna</em>, study of the Vedas, truthfulness, and active service to humanity.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Shuddhi (Purification) Movement:</strong> To counter aggressive Christian and Islamic proselytization, the Arya Samaj launched the <em>Shuddhi</em> movement—conducting Vedic purificatory rites to reconvert former Hindus and integrate lower-caste untouchables into the mainstream Vedic fold with the sacred thread (<em>Yajnopavita</em>) and Vedic Gayatri mantra recitation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Gaurakshini Sabhas (1882):</strong> Established cow-protection societies and authored the treatise <em>Gokarunanidhi</em>, promoting agricultural cattle preservation.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>3. The 1893 Split &amp; Educational Revolution</h3>\n<p>Following Dayananda's death in Ajmer in 1883, the Arya Samaj split in <strong>1893</strong> over dietary ethics (vegetarianism vs. non-vegetarianism) and educational philosophy:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The College Party (Moderates):</strong> Led by <strong>Lala Hansraj</strong> and <strong>Lala Lajpat Rai</strong>. Favored modern English-medium education combined with Vedic ethics. They founded the <strong>Dayanand Anglo-Vedic (DAV) School</strong> at Lahore in 1886, which grew into a colossal network of premier colleges across India.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Gurukul Party (Conservatives):</strong> Led by <strong>Swami Shraddhanand</strong> (Mahatma Munshi Ram) and Pandit Lekh Ram. Insisted on strict vegetarianism and ancient Gurukul-style residential education in Sanskrit. They established the historic <strong>Gurukul Kangri</strong> at Haridwar in 1902.</li>\n</ul>\n",
