            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Origins of Theosophy: New York to Adyar</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Theosophical Society</strong> was founded in <strong>New York City in November 1875</strong> by <strong>Madame Helena Petrovna Blavatsky</strong> (a Russian occultist and philosopher) and <strong>Colonel Henry Steel Olcott</strong> (an American veteran and lawyer). The term <em>Theosophy</em> is derived from Greek <em>Theosophia</em> (Divine Wisdom):</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The Shift to India (1879–1882):</strong> Realizing that the roots of ancient occult wisdom lay in the East, Blavatsky and Olcott arrived in Bombay in 1879. In <strong>1882</strong>, they established the permanent international headquarters of the Society at <strong>Adyar</strong>, on the outskirts of Madras.</li>\n  <li><strong>Philosophy and Impact:</strong> Theosophy drew profoundly from the <strong>Upanishads</strong>, <strong>Advaita Vedanta</strong>, the <strong>Bhagavad Gita</strong>, and Buddhist doctrines of <em>Karma</em> and <em>Samsara</em> (reincarnation). At a historic moment when British missionaries and colonial educators looked down upon Indian culture, the spectacle of distinguished Western scholars vigorously defending the antiquity and sublime philosophy of Hinduism revived immense national self-respect and cultural pride among the educated Indian middle class.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. Annie Besant: The Spiritual and Political Dynamo</h3>\n<p>Born in London of Irish descent, <strong>Annie Besant</strong> (1847–1933) had been a prominent crusader for secularism, birth control, and Fabian socialism alongside George Bernard Shaw. Embracing Theosophy under Madame Blavatsky, Besant arrived in India in <strong>1893</strong> and adopted it as her sacred motherland, declaring: <em>'India is the motherland of my soul.'</em></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>President of the Society:</strong> Following Colonel Olcott's death in 1907, Annie Besant was elected international President of the Theosophical Society, leading it from Adyar for over a quarter of a century.</li>\n  <li><strong>Translation of the Gita:</strong> Besant translated the <em>Bhagavad Gita</em> into beautiful English under the title <strong>The Lord's Song</strong>, popularizing it globally.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Central Hindu College, Banaras (1898):</strong> In 1898, Besant established the <em>Central Hindu College</em> at Kamachha, Banaras. Her goal was to combine modern European scientific instruction with classical Hindu character building and religious ethics. In 1916, she generously gifted the college to <strong>Pandit Madan Mohan Malaviya</strong>, where it became the foundational core of the world-renowned <strong>Banaras Hindu University (BHU)</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Political Crusade:</strong> In 1916, Besant plunged into the national struggle, establishing the <strong>All India Home Rule League</strong> and publishing newspapers <em>New India</em> and <em>Commonweal</em>. In 1917, she was elected the <strong>first woman President of the Indian National Congress</strong> at Calcutta.</li>\n</ol>\n",
