            "title": "Islamic Reform Movements: Aligarh Movement (Sir Syed Ahmad Khan), Deoband School & Wahabi/Faraizi",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Sir Syed Ahmad Khan &amp; The Aligarh Movement</h3>\n<p>Following the crushing of the 1857 Revolt, the British viewed the Muslim community with deep suspicion, marginalizing them from government administration and military ranks. Into this crisis stepped <strong>Sir Syed Ahmad Khan</strong> (1817–1898), a senior judicial officer in Company service:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Modernizing Islamic Thought:</strong> Sir Syed argued that the decline of the Muslim community was caused by blind adherence to medieval scholasticism and rejection of modern English education. He maintained that true Islam was fully compatible with modern science and reason, stating: <em>'The Word of God (the Quran) cannot be in contradiction with the Work of God (Nature).'</em></li>\n  <li><strong>Scientific Society (1864) &amp; Tahzib-ul-Akhlaq (1870):</strong> In 1864, he established the <em>Scientific Society</em> at Ghazipur (later shifted to Aligarh) to translate Western scientific, mathematical, and historical treatises into Urdu. In 1870, he launched the influential Urdu monthly journal <strong>Tahzib-ul-Akhlaq</strong> (Refinement of Manners/Morals) to combat social evils, purdah, and religious fatalism.</li>\n  <li><strong>The MAO College (1875):</strong> In 1875, Sir Syed established the <strong>Muhammadan Anglo-Oriental (MAO) College</strong> at Aligarh (with the foundation stone laid by Viceroy Lord Lytton in 1877). Modeled on Oxford and Cambridge, it taught modern Western arts and sciences alongside Islamic theology. In 1920, it was chartered as <strong>Aligarh Muslim University (AMU)</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Political Stance:</strong> Sir Syed urged Indian Muslims to shun the newly founded Indian National Congress (1885). Believing Muslims were educationally backward and a numerical minority, he feared democratic majority rule would disadvantage them, counseling unswerving loyalty to the British Crown. In 1888, along with Raja Shiv Prasad of Banaras, he founded the <em>United Indian Patriotic Association</em> to oppose the Congress.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. The Deoband School (Dar-ul-Uloom, 1866)</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Deoband Movement</strong> stood in stark ideological contrast to the Aligarh school. Founded in 1866 at Deoband (Saharanpur, UP) by orthodox theologians <strong>Muhammad Qasim Nanautawi</strong> and <strong>Rashid Ahmad Gangohi</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Revivalist Purpose:</strong> Dedicated to the pure teaching of the Quran and Hadith, rejecting Western education, modern science, and British cultural influence.</li>\n  <li><strong>Militant Anti-Imperialism:</strong> Unlike Sir Syed's pro-British policy, Deoband upheld fierce anti-colonial resistance. In 1888, the Deoband authorities issued a famous <strong>fatwa</strong> urging Muslims to cooperate wholeheartedly with the Indian National Congress and condemning Sir Syed's Patriotic Association. Leaders like <strong>Maulana Abul Kalam Azad</strong> and <strong>Mahmud-ul-Hasan</strong> (who led the Silk Letter Conspiracy) carried this anti-colonial legacy.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>3. The Wahabi &amp; Faraizi Movements</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The Wahabi Movement:</strong> Initiated in India by <strong>Syed Ahmad Barelvi</strong> of Rae Bareli, inspired by the teachings of Shah Waliullah of Delhi. It sought to purify Islam of Hindu accretions and restore Islamic political rule. Syed Ahmad declared India <em>Dar-ul-Harb</em> (Land of War) and established an armed base at Sittana on the North-West Frontier. Following his death at the Battle of Balakot in 1831, <strong>Patna</strong> became the secret administrative and financial nerve center under brothers Wilayat Ali and Inayat Ali, directing underground resistance against the British until crushed in the 1860s.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Faraizi Movement:</strong> Founded in 1818 in Eastern Bengal by <strong>Haji Shariatullah</strong>, emphasizing the mandatory duties (<em>Fara'iz</em>) of Islam. Under his son, <strong>Dudu Mian</strong> (Muhsinuddin Ahmad), it became an egalitarian peasant rebellion against oppressive Hindu zamindars and European indigo planters, declaring that <em>'Land belongs to God, and no one has the right to demand rent for it.'</em></li>\n</ul>\n",
