            "title": "Social Reform Legislation: Sati Abolition (1829), Female Infanticide, Age of Consent & Sharda Act (1929)",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Abolition of Female Infanticide &amp; Slavery</h3>\n<p>In the late 18th and 19th centuries, British administrators encountered deeply entrenched barbaric social practices:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Female Infanticide:</strong> Widely practiced among certain Rajput and Bedian clans who considered female children a financial and prestige liability. The colonial administration declared female infanticide murder under <strong>Bengal Regulation XXI of 1795</strong> and <strong>Regulation III of 1804</strong>. In 1870, the <em>Female Infanticide Prevention Act</em> made birth registration and surveillance mandatory in vulnerable districts.</li>\n  <li><strong>Abolition of Slavery (Act V of 1843):</strong> Enacted under Governor-General <strong>Lord Ellenborough</strong>, Act V of 1843 declared that courts would no longer recognize any claim to the property or labor of any individual on grounds of slavery, effectively emancipating millions of domestic and agrarian slaves.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. The Phulmoni Dasi Tragedy &amp; Age of Consent Act (1891)</h3>\n<p>Under the Indian Penal Code of 1860, the age of consent for sexual relations had been fixed at an absurdly low <strong>10 years</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>The Tragedy (1889):</strong> In Calcutta, an 11-year-old child bride named <strong>Phulmoni Dasi</strong> died in agony from horrific internal injuries inflicted by her 35-year-old husband, Hari Mohan Maiti. Because she was over 10 years old, the husband could not be charged with rape under the law.</li>\n  <li><strong>Behramji Malabari's Crusade:</strong> Parsi journalist and social reformer <strong>Behramji M. Malabari</strong> (editor of the <em>Indian Spectator</em>) traveled to England and published his explosive 1884 tract <em>Notes on Infant Marriage and Enforced Widowhood</em>, mobilizing doctors, women reformers, and the British Parliament.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Age of Consent Act of 1891:</strong> The colonial government passed the bill, raising the minimum age of consent for girls from <strong>10 to 12 years</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Tilak Controversy:</strong> <strong>Bal Gangadhar Tilak</strong> opposed the bill. Tilak argued not that child marriage was good, but that foreign, Christian British rulers had no moral or legal jurisdiction to interfere in the sacred domestic sacraments of Hindu families; reforms must come from internal societal evolution, not foreign penal statutes. Reformers like M.G. Ranade and R.G. Bhandarkar forcefully supported the Act.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>3. The Sharda Act (Child Marriage Restraint Act of 1929)</h3>\n<p>Despite earlier laws, child marriage remained rampant. In the late 1920s, <strong>Rai Sahib Harbilas Sharda</strong>, an enlightened Arya Samajist, historian, and legislative member from Ajmer, introduced a private member's bill to prohibit child marriages:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The Child Marriage Restraint Act of 1929:</strong> Popularly celebrated as the <strong>Sharda Act</strong>, it came into force on <strong>1 April 1930</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Provisions:</strong> It established the statutory minimum age of marriage at <strong>14 years for girls and 18 years for boys</strong>, applying uniformly to all British Indian subjects regardless of religion. It imposed fines and imprisonment on parents, guardians, and priests who solemnized child marriages.</li>\n</ul>\n",
