            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Pandita Ramabai: The Rebel Scholar</h3>\n<p>Born in Karnataka into an itinerant Chitpavan Brahmin family, <strong>Pandita Ramabai</strong> (1858–1922) memorized 20,000 Sanskrit verses. Arriving in Calcutta in 1878, the learned pandits of Calcutta University were astonished by her mastery of Sanskrit grammar and philosophy, formally conferring upon her the highest titles of <strong>Pandita</strong> and <strong>Saraswati</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Breaking Caste Barriers:</strong> Ramabai married an educated Bengali lawyer, Bipin Behari Medhvi, who belonged to a lower Kayastha/Shudra caste. Widowed barely nineteen months later with an infant daughter, Manorama, she relocated to Pune.</li>\n  <li><strong>Arya Mahila Samaj (1882):</strong> Collaborating with Justice M.G. Ranade and Ramabai Ranade, she founded the <em>Arya Mahila Samaj</em> in 1882 to educate women and end child marriage. Giving evidence before the <strong>Hunter Education Commission in 1882</strong>, her passionate plea persuaded Queen Victoria to launch the <em>Countess of Dufferin Fund</em> to train women doctors.</li>\n  <li><strong>The High-Caste Hindu Woman (1887):</strong> Ramabai traveled to England (where she converted to Christianity) and the United States. In 1887, she published her English masterpiece, <em>The High-Caste Hindu Woman</em>, providing a devastating insider account of the physical, emotional, and social cruelties inflicted on Hindu girls and child widows.</li>\n  <li><strong>Sharada Sadan (1889) &amp; Mukti Mission (1898):</strong> In March 1889, she established <strong>Sharada Sadan</strong> (Home of Learning) in Bombay for child widows. During the catastrophic 1897 famine, she rescued over 1,500 starving girls, establishing the self-reliant <strong>Mukti Mission</strong> at Kedgaon near Pune, providing printing presses, weaving, carpentry, and medical training.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. Tarabai Shinde &amp; 'Stri Purush Tulana' (1882)</h3>\n<p>In 1882, <strong>Tarabai Shinde</strong> (1850–1910), a member of Jyotirao Phule's Satyashodhak Samaj from Berar (Maharashtra), published a 40-page Marathi pamphlet titled <strong>Stri Purush Tulana</strong> ('A Comparison Between Women and Men'):</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>The Context:</strong> A young Brahmin widow named Vijayalakshmi had been sentenced to death (commuted to life transportation) for aborting an illegitimate pregnancy resulting from sexual exploitation, while the man faced zero social or legal penalty.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Radical Critique:</strong> Tarabai wrote a searing feminist manifesto dismantling the patriarchal myth that women are inherently weak, fickle, and deceitful. She exposed male hypocrisy: <em>'Men commit every sin—adultery, drunkenness, desertion—yet retain their caste purity; while a woman who takes a step outside is burned alive or banished. Are women not human beings like you?'</em> It is acknowledged as modern India's first published feminist work.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>3. Begum Rokeya Sakhawat Hossain: Light of Bengal</h3>\n<p>Born into an orthodox zamindar family in Pairaband (Rangpur, Bengal), <strong>Begum Rokeya</strong> (1880–1932) was secluded behind rigid <em>purdah</em>, but secretly learned English and Bengali at night with her brother's help. Married to an enlightened magistrate, Syed Sakhawat Hossain:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Sakhawat Memorial Girls' School (1909):</strong> Using her deceased husband's money, she founded the school at Bhagalpur in 1909 with just five students, moving it to Calcutta in 1911. She went door-to-door pleading with conservative Muslim families to send their daughters to school. In 1916, she founded the <strong>Anjuman-e-Khawateen-e-Islam</strong> (Muslim Women's Association).</li>\n  <li><strong>Sultana's Dream (1905):</strong> Published in the English journal <em>The Indian Ladies' Magazine</em> of Madras, this visionary feminist sci-fi novella depicted <strong>Ladyland</strong>—a utopian world where women scientists invent solar energy, flying cars, and cloud-harvesting, while warmongering men are secluded in the domestic quarters (the <em>Murdana</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n",
